 <aside class="mb-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-6 lg:sticky lg:top-24">
 <h2 class="mb-4 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">In this article</h2>
 <ol class="space-y-2 font-mono text-sm text-ash">
 <li><a href="#how-they-differ" class="hover:text-gold-600">1. How the two inks actually differ</a></li>
 <li><a href="#plastisol-pros" class="hover:text-gold-600">2. Where plastisol may fit workwear</a></li>
 <li><a href="#water-based-pros" class="hover:text-gold-600">3. Where water-based wins</a></li>
 <li><a href="#by-fabric" class="hover:text-gold-600">4. Behavior by fabric (cotton vs poly)</a></li>
 <li><a href="#vegas-conditions" class="hover:text-gold-600">5. The Vegas heat factor</a></li>
 <li><a href="#decision" class="hover:text-gold-600">6. Decision tree by garment</a></li>
 </ol>
 </aside>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Ask which ink and curing process are planned for your job. The garment, ink, cure, and laundering set how it wears, so be wary of any month or wash-count promise.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Here's the working contractor's breakdown of plastisol vs water-based ink: what each one is, where each one wins, and how to spec for your crews instead of getting whatever the shop defaults to.
 </p>

 <h2 id="how-they-differ" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">1. How the Two Inks Actually Differ</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Both inks are pushed through a screen and onto fabric the same way. The chemistry is what changes.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Plastisol</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A PVC-based ink suspended in plasticizer. It doesn't dry. It cures. The ink sits on top of the fabric and locks when heated through a cure tunnel at 320°F. Once cured, it's essentially a flexible plastic film bonded to the shirt.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Water-based</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Pigment + binder + water. The water evaporates during cure, leaving the pigment soaked into the fabric fibers themselves. It dyes the cotton instead of sitting on top of it.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 That fundamental difference (sits on top vs sinks in) drives almost every pro and con of each method.
 </p>

 <h2 id="plastisol-pros" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">2. Where Plastisol May Fit Workwear</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Plastisol is a common choice for construction work shirts. Here's why:
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Opacity on dark fabric</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Plastisol is fully opaque. A white logo on a navy tee looks white: bright, clean, no fabric showing through. Water-based on dark fabric requires a white underbase first, which adds steps and cost.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Color match precision</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 The ink, the garment, and the artwork set how close a color match gets. Bighorn sends you the color and a written proof before production.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Wash durability</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Properly cured plastisol can be durable, and the cure, garment, and laundry process set how long it lasts. Confirm compatibility with the garment manufacturer and any commercial laundry provider. Protective apparel requires a separate employer and manufacturer review.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Bold graphics</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Big back prints, full-front graphics, bold type: plastisol delivers them with crisp edges and consistent ink lay. The opacity and ink film make the design read from across a job site.
 </p>

 <div class="my-10 border-l-4 border-gold-500 bg-panel p-6">
 <p class="mb-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">One common option</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Plastisol is one common candidate for opaque graphics. We confirm the garment, ink, cure, color target, and care before you approve.
 </p>
 </div>

 <h2 id="water-based-pros" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">3. Where Water-Based Wins</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Water-based is a different tool that may fit specific garments, artwork, and hand-feel goals:
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Soft hand-feel</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Water-based printing can create a softer hand on compatible garments. The ink, fabric, underbase, and cure shape the result, so review a sample or a product-specific proof.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Vintage / faded look</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Some water-based systems can support a softer or vintage-style result. The garment, ink, process, and care set color and aging.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Hot conditions, breathability</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 A large surface print can change local hand feel and air movement. Compare print area, ink deposit, garment fabric, and a sample when comfort matters. Decoration does not replace an employer heat-illness plan.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Light fabric on light shirt</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Water-based ink may suit some light-on-light or tonal designs. The garment, ink, artwork, and cure set the final color and feel.
 </p>

 <h2 id="by-fabric" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">4. Behavior by Fabric: Cotton vs Poly</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 This is where ink choice gets technical. The two inks behave very differently on the two main shirt fabrics.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">100% cotton</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Both inks work, but water-based is at its best here. The cotton fibers absorb the pigment and lock it in. Plastisol works fine but doesn't have the soft-hand advantage.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">100% polyester (hi-vis, performance)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Polyester can require ink and curing choices that control dye migration. We confirm a process that fits your garment and artwork.
 </p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold text-charcoal">Cotton-poly blends (50/50, 60/40)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Blends require product-specific testing because fiber content, dyes, ink, additives, and cure affect the result. We recommend a process that fits and put it in writing.
 </p>

 <h2 id="vegas-conditions" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">5. The Vegas Heat Factor</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Hot storage can affect some decorated products. Ask for curing, packing, storage, and care guidance on your order.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Ink and curing requirements depend on the garment and ink system. We confirm the process for your order.
 </p>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Water-based results also come down to the ink, cure, garment, and care. Ask for product-specific guidance when apparel may remain in a hot vehicle.
 </p>

 <h2 id="decision" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">6. Decision Tree by Garment</h2>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 Here's how we usually call it for crew apparel:
 </p>

 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Crew tees (cotton, daily wear)</strong> → plastisol if you want bold, water-based if you want premium</li>
 <li><strong>Hi-vis polyester</strong>: employer selects the protective garment; manufacturer instructions and the production partner govern eligible decoration</li>
 <li><strong>Cotton-poly blend tees</strong>: test for dye migration and confirm a compatible ink and cure</li>
 <li><strong>Hoodies and crewnecks</strong>: compare ink systems by fabric, artwork, hand feel, opacity, and care</li>
 <li><strong>Premium brand merch (giveaways, recruiting)</strong> → water-based for the soft-hand premium feel</li>
 <li><strong>Polos</strong>: compare embroidery and other eligible methods for the exact product (see <a href="/screen-printing-vs-embroidery-construction-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">screen vs embroidery breakdown</a>)</li>
 <li><strong>Flame-resistant shirts</strong>: employer selection and current manufacturer decoration instructions control; do not assume an ink preserves protection</li>
 </ul>

 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 For more details, see our <a href="/services/screen-printing-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline hover:text-gold-600">screen printing service page</a>. Bighorn lines up the ink and the garment for production. Available methods depend on the partner and product.
 </p>

 <div class="my-12 border border-hairline bg-panel p-8 text-center">
 <h3 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-extrabold uppercase tracking-tight text-charcoal">Want a recommendation on your specific gear?</h3>
